DEV file number 215-0229, Bellevue Pond.

33
00:03:07,173 --> 00:03:23,745
The Massachusetts Department of Conservation and Recreation has filed a notice of intent for the implementation of an invasive plant management in the Bellevue Pond section of the Millisex Fells Reservation within jurisdictional areas, inland bank, vegetated wetland in the 200 Riverfront area, Floydsbrook, and Medford Bass.

34
00:03:24,406 --> 00:03:28,067
This film, the filing is being submitted as an ecological restoration limited project.

35
00:03:30,208 --> 00:03:36,109
Good evening, my name is Alia Chandi and I am an Inland Ecologist with the Department of Conservation and Recreation.

36
00:03:37,170 --> 00:03:45,632
I first wanted to thank Dennis for all his assistance with this filing and you can slow down if you're, you know, reading everything like the micromachine guy.

37
00:03:47,873 --> 00:03:48,113
Never.

38
00:03:49,813 --> 00:03:59,076
As Dennis mentioned, we are filing a project for ecological restoration at the Bellevue Pond area of the Middlesex Fells Reservation.

39
00:03:59,096 --> 00:04:03,997
Dennis, do you mind giving me access to be able to share screen so I can share a map?

40
00:04:04,157 --> 00:04:04,497
Thank you.

41
00:04:09,318 --> 00:04:09,818
You're all set.

42
00:04:10,519 --> 00:04:10,779
All right.

43
00:04:14,099 --> 00:04:15,780
Can everybody see the map that's on the screen?

44
00:04:16,335 --> 00:04:16,575
Yes.

45
00:04:17,116 --> 00:04:19,257
Okay, so this is the project location.

46
00:04:19,838 --> 00:04:24,242
The project area includes the red and yellow polygons on this map.

47
00:04:25,162 --> 00:04:26,664
So here is Bellevue Pond.

48
00:04:26,804 --> 00:04:33,249
This is 93 South and the 28 Rotary, and then we have South Border Road.

49
00:04:34,030 --> 00:04:42,417
So the project area was selected based on an assessment that DCR completed back in 2017, where DCR hired consultants to do a

50
00:04:45,279 --> 00:05:04,415
a GIS desktop study that looked at different overlays of available mass GIS information, including Biomap2, Priority Habitat, Estimated Habitat, Vernal Pools, wetlands, and provide DCRI priority of areas that would require invasive surveys and invasive management.

51
00:05:05,315 --> 00:05:09,859
This is one of the areas that was highlighted for the north region of the DCR parks.

52
00:05:10,920 --> 00:05:12,481
There was another area within

53
00:05:13,682 --> 00:05:19,285
Middlesex Falls reservation which is the with pill section in Stoneham right across the street from the zoo New England.

54
00:05:19,805 --> 00:05:25,208
We did have our hearing with Stoneham yesterday, and we were able to close and they'll be issuing an order of conditions.

55
00:05:25,708 --> 00:05:30,431
It was pretty much similar filing obviously with different wetland resource areas.

56
00:05:32,031 --> 00:05:35,212
So again, this is being filed as an eco restoration project.

57
00:05:35,352 --> 00:05:44,075
Our goal is to do invasive species management in order to further the protection of the act specifics to wildlife habitat.

58
00:05:45,096 --> 00:05:48,577
We also have the goal of enhancing habitat of rare species.

59
00:05:48,637 --> 00:05:52,118
We do have rare species within this area of the Middlesex Frost Reservation.

60
00:05:52,878 --> 00:06:01,702
As a result of the endangered species present, we submitted a Habitat Management Plan to Natural Heritage and the Habitat Management Plan was approved.

61
00:06:02,522 --> 00:06:09,126
So we have the exemption where invasive species management can be completed without any further review from Natural Heritage.

62
00:06:09,606 --> 00:06:14,208
And then it says a copy of both the Habitat Management Plan as well as the letter of approval from Heritage.

63
00:06:16,068 --> 00:06:32,452
As Dennis mentioned, the resource areas that are going to be impacted as a result of the invasive species management include BBW, Riverfront Area, Bank, and then the associated buffers or the adjacent upland resource areas as referred to by the Medford Ordinance.

64
00:06:33,092 --> 00:06:38,553
The areas were delineated in March and May of this year by DCR staff as well as SWCA staff.

65
00:06:38,993 --> 00:06:48,863
SWCA is a consultant that is assisting DCR with some of the mapping and some of the other components of the NOI that DCR just didn't have the capacity to complete.

66
00:06:50,864 --> 00:07:00,133
In a visually pleasing manner, we could do it very roughly, but by allowing a consultant to assist us, we can make it look a little better.

67
00:07:00,773 --> 00:07:11,083
And I did want to point out that this project is the result of a partnership matching funds grant with the Friends of the Fells, so this is a partnership between the Friends of the Fells and DCR.

68
00:07:15,158 --> 00:07:27,842
So, while we were doing the wetland delineations we also completed additional invasive species surveys to kind of bolster the information that had been completed in 2017 because invasive species as we all know spread very quickly.

69
00:07:28,622 --> 00:07:30,683
So we wanted to make sure that we had accurate data.

70
00:07:31,303 --> 00:07:37,709
We didn't provide the specific invasive species location information to the commission with the filing just because the map would look a little messy.

71
00:07:38,129 --> 00:07:46,517
But I do have our Esri online collector map available to show the commission where the invasive species are located, which include

72
00:07:47,037 --> 00:07:49,960
all of the resource areas and buffers previously noted.

73
00:07:50,681 --> 00:08:02,753
Based on the wetland delineations and our observation of invasive species, we were able to capture a percent cover of invasive species and that's how we were able to determine impacts to each resource area.

74
00:08:05,534 --> 00:08:11,659
So as part of the eco and logical restoration project, DCR plans on submitting annual plans to the commission.

75
00:08:11,679 --> 00:08:20,466
The annual plan will include a summary of all activities completed during the prior year, as well as proposed activities to be completed the next year.

76
00:08:20,526 --> 00:08:23,748
And again, our fiscal year is different than the calendar year.

77
00:08:23,788 --> 00:08:31,655
So we're looking at July 1st to June 30th is really our implementation time for invasive species management.

78
00:08:32,335 --> 00:08:47,862
invasive species management is going to be completed by contractors hired by DCR, volunteers as well as DCR staff, and attachment E provides all the alternative methods that DCR looks for approval from the conservation commission from an implementation basis.

79
00:08:47,902 --> 00:08:52,103
So we have everything from manual to mechanical to herbicide treatment.

80
00:08:52,504 --> 00:08:57,826
And I also have a decision tree that I can share with the commission that explains how DCR makes a decision as to

81
00:08:58,346 --> 00:09:00,688
how to treat individual invasive species.

82
00:09:01,328 --> 00:09:04,370
Unfortunately, a lot of it has to do with funding.

83
00:09:04,530 --> 00:09:15,417
If we have funding available to hire contractors and herbicide applications are the most scientifically proven way of managing an invasive, that's what we will be implementing.

84
00:09:15,977 --> 00:09:21,241
However, it's going to be a limited herbicides where we're going to be cutting and painting.

85
00:09:21,281 --> 00:09:26,304
We're not talking about doing any broadcast spraying or boom spraying or any sort of

86
00:09:26,824 --> 00:09:33,085
herbicide application that could impact non-targeted species, so non-invasive species.

87
00:09:34,585 --> 00:09:40,226
So again, all impacts associated with the project are specific to the invasive species management that we're proposing.

88
00:09:40,706 --> 00:09:49,428
And we are requesting a five-year order condition just because as you may all know, invasive species management is kind of an internal project.

89
00:09:50,988 --> 00:09:54,469
So we're hoping to be able to get five years and then continue on in the future.

90
00:09:55,329 --> 00:10:04,039
especially with our partners that are supporting this project, and I believe that Myra and the Friends of the Fell submitted letters of support.

91
00:10:04,539 --> 00:10:06,662
They're going to be two of the groups that are going to be working with us.

92
00:10:07,663 --> 00:10:16,953
And then our hope is that, you know, within the first three to five years, we'll be able to implement some restoration techniques, which will be planting of native species.

93
00:10:17,853 --> 00:10:25,878
And we will only be planting native species as listed in the Massachusetts vascular county, vascular plant county checklist.

94
00:10:26,158 --> 00:10:32,782
So they're gonna be very specific to this county, as opposed to other native species that may not be present in this area.

95
00:10:33,403 --> 00:10:38,806
And we would also be providing that information to the commission prior to any implementation.

96
00:10:38,826 --> 00:10:41,208
So we would be providing that as part of the annual plans.

97
00:10:42,489 --> 00:10:43,610
And I've been talking a lot.

98
00:10:44,250 --> 00:10:53,299
So I don't know if there's any specific questions from the commission or if you want me to go through any other specifics that were provided in the notice of intent.

99
00:10:57,784 --> 00:10:59,866
I would like to share more about that decision.

100
00:11:02,228 --> 00:11:02,469
Sure.

101
00:11:03,209 --> 00:11:03,389
Yes.

102
00:11:04,412 --> 00:11:26,222
So this is the decision tree for DCR again we categorize well it's there's different groups of people that are different groups or categories of personnel that are going to be conducting the work and they're listed on the right hand side, but we do divide the type of invasive species management implementation based on the type of vegetation that it is so you have your.

103
00:11:26,762 --> 00:11:42,290
your non-woodies and then that spread by fruit or seed, or you have your non-woodies that can spread by fruit, seed and plant fragmentation, such as Japanese knotweed, which we unfortunately have a lot of in that Bellevue Pond area.

104
00:11:43,351 --> 00:11:52,078
Then we also have vines that spread by fruit and seed, and then vines that spread by fruit, seed, and plant fragmentation, and that includes your Asiatic bittersweet.

105
00:11:52,919 --> 00:12:06,331
And then you have all your woody shrubs, which is probably the bulk of the invasive vegetation that is present at Bellevue Pond, second to that Japanese knotweed stand that everybody can enjoy from South Border Road as you're driving by.

106
00:12:08,838 --> 00:12:14,605
Now, didn't they have some success last time with removing Japanese knotweed with doing the grubbing?

107
00:12:14,625 --> 00:12:16,368
They had like a grubbing machine.

108
00:12:16,408 --> 00:12:17,629
I don't see any of that here.

109
00:12:17,649 --> 00:12:21,855
I see hand pulling, but not anything about grubbing, getting the roots out.

110
00:12:22,436 --> 00:12:35,130
Yep, so grubbing requires additional reviews for any grubbing on DCR property because everything can be archaeologically sensitive or culturally sensitive so we're trying to get away from the grubbing.

111
00:12:35,490 --> 00:12:42,557
Now the grubbing requires a lot of effort to, in terms of the, it's not just grubbing and digging, and then it's continued

112
00:12:43,138 --> 00:12:46,321
continues monitoring and removing more and more of those pieces.

113
00:12:46,781 --> 00:12:53,086
And then the question comes in and how do you dispose those pieces because they're all those fragments have the potential of reproducing.

114
00:12:53,947 --> 00:12:57,149
So I'm not aware that DCR is doing specifically grubbing.

115
00:12:58,390 --> 00:12:59,812
But what we are proposing is

116
00:13:01,753 --> 00:13:04,696
cutting, continuous cutting, which has been a proven method.

117
00:13:04,756 --> 00:13:10,181
If you cut four to five times a year for the next 10, 15 years, it kind of depletes its resources.

118
00:13:10,981 --> 00:13:16,186
We're also proposing to try this new technique that is used in England.

119
00:13:16,206 --> 00:13:27,937
I don't know if the commission is familiar with Japanese knotweed impacts in England where you can't even get a mortgage if you have Japanese knotweed in your backyard because it impairs the structural stability of a home.

120
00:13:29,117 --> 00:13:38,481
What they've been using is like chicken wire that you put down on the ground and the knotweed tries to grow through it when it's little shoots and then it chokes itself out.

121
00:13:38,582 --> 00:13:44,144
Other plants that are herbaceous can grow through the mesh, but the Japanese knotweed just doesn't make it.

122
00:13:45,425 --> 00:13:56,890
The Japanese knotweed in this location specifically is located right above the bank of a perennial stream, so we would also not want to grow up in order to protect the stream from any potential sedimentation during or after

123
00:13:57,992 --> 00:13:59,086
the activity is completed.

124
00:14:00,848 --> 00:14:09,292
There's also solarization, and I shared a document yesterday with the Stonehenge Conservation Commission from New Hampshire.

125
00:14:09,332 --> 00:14:18,117
They have a really good guide for Japanese knotweed management, and their solarization techniques are really good, and I can share that with this commission as well.

126
00:14:18,337 --> 00:14:28,442
It actually tells you how to layer the fabric with just enough soil and doing some plantings over it, what the thickness needs to be in order for the knotweed not to break through the fabric.

127
00:14:34,718 --> 00:14:39,344
You're also talking about herbicides, can you be specific as to which herbicides you're going to use?

128
00:14:40,045 --> 00:14:52,019
Yep, so that is provided in attachment E of the filing and each of the herbicide is highlighted in a different color so that's a hyperlink that you can actually click on and get the SDS sheet as well as the

129
00:14:55,540 --> 00:14:57,062
the label, the herbicide label.

130
00:14:57,102 --> 00:15:00,805
So we're looking at some glyphosate and triclopyr are the main two.

131
00:15:01,305 --> 00:15:14,817
We also are proposing imazapyr if we were to find some naphleid, which we have not seen in this area yet, but it is present in other areas of the reservation.

132
00:15:16,646 --> 00:15:22,870
And again, the technique that we would be using, if we use a backpack sprayer, is very targeted with a low flow spray.

133
00:15:23,310 --> 00:15:27,453
So it's only the leaf of the plant that we're trying to target and not anything else.

134
00:15:28,013 --> 00:15:34,597
Otherwise, we're gonna be, for the woodies, you cut and then you paint the stump, so there's really no herbicide that gets into any of the other areas.

135
00:15:35,358 --> 00:15:43,623
And then for phragmites, which we're not proposing for Bellevue Pond specifically, because there isn't any, which is great,

136
00:15:44,023 --> 00:15:49,486
but there is in the Stoneham section, we would do hand wiping.

137
00:15:49,526 --> 00:15:53,629
This would be only applied by licensed applicators.

138
00:15:53,929 --> 00:15:56,690
No one else would be allowed to do any herbicide applications.

139
00:15:56,750 --> 00:15:59,392
No staff, no volunteers, just licensed herbicide applicators.

140
00:16:06,016 --> 00:16:07,517
Thank you for that presentation, Ally.

141
00:16:08,978 --> 00:16:11,179
Does anybody else from the commission have questions?

142
00:16:15,415 --> 00:16:17,096
I have one that I don't know if you can answer.

143
00:16:17,116 --> 00:16:22,858
I see that there is a natural heritage and endangered species polygon here.

144
00:16:23,318 --> 00:16:25,599
Can you share with us what species that is?

145
00:16:26,539 --> 00:16:29,781
Unfortunately, I cannot share that information, at least not in a public meeting.

146
00:16:30,801 --> 00:16:36,123
However, I don't think it's changed since Biomap was published.

147
00:16:36,143 --> 00:16:40,645
If you do some detective work, I think you'll be able to figure out what it is.

148
00:16:40,665 --> 00:16:41,886
I'm sure.

149
00:16:41,966 --> 00:16:42,246
Thank you.

150
00:16:49,526 --> 00:16:53,568
Okay, seeing no further questions, then do I have a motion from the commission?

151
00:16:58,491 --> 00:17:00,532
Or I guess I should back up.

152
00:17:04,795 --> 00:17:06,516
And we can talk about it among ourselves.

153
00:17:09,357 --> 00:17:13,100
I'm in favor of issuing an order of conditions for this project.

154
00:17:13,120 --> 00:17:17,142
Does anybody have any other opinions or anything to add?

155
00:17:19,625 --> 00:17:21,226
Heidi, I'm totally supportive of it.

156
00:17:21,246 --> 00:17:23,787
I thought it was an excellent presentation and very thorough.

157
00:17:25,208 --> 00:17:28,749
I also think that it needs to be done.

158
00:17:28,769 --> 00:17:39,034
I'm very happy to see that there's a conjunction of different organizations while supporting the same idea with the same goals.

159
00:17:39,294 --> 00:17:40,575
That really makes me happy to see that.

160
00:17:40,975 --> 00:17:43,096
I did have a question about these red lines though.

161
00:17:43,116 --> 00:17:45,737
I wanted to come back to that, but it's not really that important.

162
00:17:45,797 --> 00:17:47,838
I just want to know what these red lines then were depicting.

163
00:17:48,802 --> 00:18:02,272
Is that the extent of your... Yes, so for some reason, when we planned the project for surveying purposes, we divided this up into two different survey areas as opposed to one large survey area.

164
00:18:02,853 --> 00:18:08,157
It could have been because I didn't know if we were going to have enough funding to complete all of it with the SWCA.

165
00:18:08,978 --> 00:18:12,641
So both the yellow and the red are the project areas.

166
00:18:14,459 --> 00:18:30,884
So the yellow extends a little further, so it's technically 20 feet out from the trail that surrounds Bellevue Pond, 20 feet out from the trail that goes up to Wrights Tower, and then everything south of Wrights Tower to South Border Road and 93.

167
00:18:31,624 --> 00:18:34,665
And Bellevue Pond itself, you know, because it's getting pulled in.

168
00:18:35,945 --> 00:18:37,706
That's not part of this, is that right?

169
00:18:38,318 --> 00:18:40,779
So we're not doing any aquatic treatments, no.

170
00:18:41,339 --> 00:18:44,480
No, so no purple loosestrife, which is present in this pond.

171
00:18:44,500 --> 00:18:48,802
And like I said, there's no phragmites there, but we're not applying anything to water.

172
00:18:48,822 --> 00:18:52,323
Or on the area of the water, I mean.

173
00:18:52,383 --> 00:18:54,884
The area of the open water area, exactly.

174
00:18:54,904 --> 00:18:55,324
Right, okay.

175
00:18:55,344 --> 00:18:57,525
So will that be coming up with another proposal later on?

176
00:19:02,444 --> 00:19:13,710
So, to be honest with you, the only invasive that I saw in Bellevue pond was purple loosestrife, and I do remember seeing the gala reseller beetles which are the bio control, which really keep it in check.

177
00:19:13,770 --> 00:19:16,851
There's a really nice diversity of plants in this pond.

178
00:19:18,392 --> 00:19:21,434
and in the spillway, which is nice to see.

179
00:19:21,914 --> 00:19:25,536
So I don't think that will be necessary, but we'll definitely keep an eye on it.

180
00:19:25,576 --> 00:19:33,141
And if something does show up, then we will reach out to the commission and get permission to treat it or remove it as much as possible.

181
00:19:34,241 --> 00:19:35,962
No water is just not here, which is awesome.

182
00:19:36,823 --> 00:19:40,505
Okay, so there's nothing coming, you know, it's not like- No.

183
00:19:40,605 --> 00:19:40,805
Okay.

184
00:19:41,005 --> 00:19:41,165
Nope.

185
00:19:45,788 --> 00:19:47,429
So I'm happy moving forward with this too.

186
00:19:50,107 --> 00:19:51,768
Just I have a couple of things to just add.

187
00:19:51,808 --> 00:19:54,570
